To install the interface between the F90 SkyAx and the F77 SkyAx_odd:
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The wrapper routine resides in the 'source_f90' of the 'SkyAx'
directory.
To install it go to the subdirectory 'source_f90' and issue
'./complink_wrapper'. As a result you obtain the executable 'wrapper.exe'
at the directory one level before 'source_f90'.

Go to the subdirectory 'source_odd' and issue 'make skyifc' if you have
an Intel compiler or 'make skyf77' if you prefer the GNU compiler. 
The result is an executable 'skyax_odd.exe' one level before 'source'.

Now define a working directory for your runs use inputs from the input directory 
into that working directory. This delivers a couple of input files
with obvious names. Then you need to link 'skyax_odd.exe' to that
working directory. It is also convenient to link 'wrapper.exe' to
the working directory. Then you are ready to go.

A run with the F77 code is performed by './wrapper.exe < for005.2D.....'
where .... means your chosen functional. Before that you edit the input 
file before that it runs the nuclear you want (entries 'nprot' and 
'nneut') and you can associate a 3-digit qualifier as usual. The output
comes on 'out_details.*' and 'ensurf.*'. 

I emphasize that this is an alpha-version which needs yet to be
tested. For even-even nuclei, we can counter-check by running the
SkyAx code with the same input and also by comparing with the 1D code
for spherical nuclei. I did that and found satisfying agreement for the 
Skyrme functionals. The Fayans functionals have still to be scrutinized.

Moreover, the UNEDF2 functional raises a subtle problem. My 1D input
indicates that UNEDF2 uses tensor spin-orbit terms. These are not
installed in neither 2D code. WE have to discuss how to deal with that.



Update 9. February 2026:
------------------------

An automatic search and scan for blocking configurations has been coded.
The tools for that are collected in 'm_blocking.f90'. The routine
'search_block' looks for all s.p. states whose quasi-particle energy is
below a given threshold 'absdiff' and stores them in a book-keeping
array. The 'absdiff' is communicated through namelist 'static' and has a 
default value of 2 MeV. The entries in the book-keeping fields are then
worked up in the order of increasing quasiparticle energies.




Update 11. February 2026:
------------------------

The energy window for selecting blocked configuration has been renamed
from 'absdiff' to 'maxexenerg'. 

A routine for computing the total angular momentum of a given s.p. state
has been implemented.

A further selector for blocked configuration has been introduced- It is
'maxamomdiff' which confines the configuration to blocked  s.p. states
whose difference between azimuthal angular momentum K and total angular
momentum J is smaller then 'maxamomdiff'. A small value of 'maxamomdiff'
is useful for nearly spherical odd-even or even-odd nuclei.



Update 30, July 2026:
---------------------

The wrapper has been extended to run also quadrupole constrained 
calculations. The additional input is the same as in SkyAx, namely
through the namelist 'constraint'. However, only a quadrupole
constraint is accepted. New output files are: 
'crankn.<name>'  = vibrational and rotational masses and widths,
'zpecor.<name>'  = vibrational and rotational ZPE and widths,
'colpar*.<name>' = the same information in a different layout 
                   (not used in the following).
The new wrapper resides in the subdirectory 'source_f90' of
the base directory of 'SkyAx' presently on the branch 'odd_develop'
of the GIT repository of 'SkyAx'.

The new wrapper program has also a built-in computation of the
zero-point energy (ZPE) corrections with topological GOA (topGOA).
The version in the full 5D triaxial collective dynamics of 
summarized in eq. (5) in EPJA 37 (2008) 343. The older version 
in reduced axial topology is summarized in eqs. (3.16-3.20) in 
PRC 60 (1999) 014316. The analyzing routine provides both results.
It reads information from '<crank.<name>' and writes to a new file
'zpetop.<name>'. This files comes along with a header explaining 
the output.

Installation and usage proceeds as follows:
The wrapper routine is called now 'wrapper_ZPE.f90'. It resides in
the subdirectory of the 'SkyAx' base directory. 
It is compiled and linked in the subdirectory 'source_f90' with 
the shell command './complink_wrapper_ZPE'. The executable
'wrapper_ZPE.exe' is then found in the 'SkyAx' base.
The wrapper can be run in any directory of your choice. First,
you have to linked the old SkyAx code to 'skyax.exe' in your
working directory. Then you can issue
'.../wrapper_ZPE.exe < for005' where "..." stands for the path
to your 'SkyAx' base and 'for005' can be an input file of your
choice, of course, in the namelist format of the new 'SkyAx'
code.

